    question: "Why do homes on Squak Mountain frequently need electrical panel upgrades for EV chargers?",
    answer: "A significant portion of Squak Mountain's residential housing stock was constructed between 1965 and 1985. These homes were typically outfitted with 100-amp or 150-amp electrical panels. Modern electric heating, heat pumps, electric water heaters, and household appliances already consume most of this available amperage. Adding a Level 2 EV charger drawing 32 to 48 continuous amps requires an official load calculation under NEC Article 220, and frequently requires upgrading the service panel to 200 amps to ensure long-term fire safety."
  },
  {
    question: "How do you run electrical wiring to detached carports on steep hillside lots?",
    answer: "Squak Mountain properties often feature steep topographic grades where the garage or parking deck is separated from the main residence by sloping terrain or exterior stairs. We utilize heavy-gauge schedule 80 PVC or rigid metal conduit for exterior runs, trenching below the local frost line or anchoring securely along solid concrete retaining walls to deliver uninterrupted 240-volt power without disrupting the native mountain vegetation and trees."
  },
  {
    question: "How does tree canopy cover affect electrical reliability on Squak Mountain?",
    answer: "Dense evergreen trees on Squak Mountain can cause overhead utility lines from Puget Sound Energy to experience intermittent line disturbances during windstorms. Falling branches can induce voltage spikes or momentary neutral drops. We strongly recommend installing whole-home Type 2 surge protection devices at your electrical panel when repairing an EV charger to protect both the charging station's internal microprocessor and your vehicle's onboard battery management system."
  },
  {
    question: "Do I need a City of Issaquah electrical permit for work on Squak Mountain?",
    answer: "Yes. All electrical work involving new 240-volt circuits, panel upgrades, or hardwired EV charging equipment on Squak Mountain requires an electrical permit. Depending on the exact address, permits are administered through the City of Issaquah Development Services Department or the Washington State Department of Labor & Industries (L&I). Our licensed electrical contractors manage the entire permitting and inspection process on your behalf."
  },
  {
    question: "What should I do if my charger shows a red error light after a winter storm?",
    answer: "Following high winds or heavy rain, red fault lights are commonly triggered by minor ground fault resistance changes, moisture ingress into exterior disconnect boxes, or temporary line voltage drops from Puget Sound Energy distribution transformers. Turn off the dedicated breaker and contact our technicians to perform an on-site insulation resistance and voltage stability test."
  }
];
